 tested. Here's what I've set up:\n\n## ✅ What's Configured\n\n**Three main scripts:**\n\n1. **`setup-git-server.sh`** - Creates a bare git repository with a post-receive hook that auto-deploys to the web server\n2. **`start-webserver.sh`** - Starts an HTTP server on port 8080 serving files from the `www/` directory\n3. **`test-workflow.sh`** - Complete end-to-end test demonstrating the workflow\n\n**Directory structure:**\n```\n/app/\n├── git-server/          # Bare git repository\n│   └── hooks/post-receive  # Auto-deployment hook\n├── www/                 # Web server files\n└── [scripts]\n```\n\n## ✅ Verified Workflow\n\nThe test confirms the full workflow works:\n1. Files pushed to the git repository\n2. Post-receive hook automatically deploys them to `www/`\n3. Web server can serve the files\n\n**Test result:** Pushed `hello.html` with content \"hello world\" and it was successfully deployed!\n\n## 🚀 To Use On Your Computer\n\n1. Copy the scripts and setup to your server\n2. Run `./setup-git-server.sh` to initialize\n3. Clone via `git clone user@server:/path/to/git-server`\n4. Make changes and push - they'll auto-deploy to port 8080\n5. Access via `curl http://server:8080/hello.html`\n\nThe setup handles the SSH login you mentioned - just point your git clone to wherever you place the `git-server` directory on your server, and the post-receive hook will handle deployment automatically.","ttft_ms":9849,"type":"result","duration_ms":140302,"uuid":"7448b052-9808-4f90-a674-73c38e06a079","ttft_stream_ms":1563,"time_to_request_ms":80,"queued_turn_count":0}
